REGIONAL REGULATION OF PANGKALPINANG CITY

NUMBER 8 OF 2020

CONCERNING

EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

MAYOR OF PANGKALPINANG,

Considering:

a. that women as national assets who play a role in the process of continuation and creation of quality generations need to obtain guarantees for the fulfillment of their rights and Protection from acts of violence and discrimination;
b. that in order to provide Protection and means of self-actualization for women in society in Pangkalpinang City, there is a need for a regional regulation governing the empowerment and protection of women;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on Empowerment and Women;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 28 of 1959 concerning Stipulation of Emergency Law Number 4 of 1959 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1959 Number 55, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1091), Emergency Law Number 5 of 1956 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1956 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1091) and Emergency Law Number 57 (Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1091) concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions including Municipal City Within the Level I Region of South Sumatra as Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1821);
3. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1974 Number 1);
4. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention The Elimination Of All Forms Of Discrimination Against Women) State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
5. Law Number 13 of 1998 concerning Elderly Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1998 Number 190, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3796);
6. Law Number 20 of 1999 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention No. 138 Concerning Minimum Age for Admission to Employment (ILO Convention Concerning Minimum Age for Admission to Employment) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835;
7. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835);
8. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279);
9. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
10. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
11. Law Number 19 of 2011 concerning Ratification of the Convention On The Rights Of Persons With Disabilities (Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities), (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 107, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5251);
12. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
13. Law Number 8 of 2016 concerning Persons with Disabilities (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 69, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5871);
14. Regional Regulation of Pangkalpinang City Number 18 of 2016 concerning the Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus of Pangkalpinang City (Regional Gazette of Pangkalpinang City of 2016 Number 18);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF PANGKALPINANG CITY

and

MAYOR OF PANGKALPINANG

Article 2

1. The Principles of Women's Empowerment are:
a. respect for human rights;
b. gender equality and justice;
c. protection of victims' rights; and
d. non-discriminatory.
2. The Objectives of Women's Empowerment and Protection are to:
a. ensure the fulfillment of women's rights so they can live and participate optimally in accordance with human dignity;
b. provide gender justice and equality;
c. provide protection and security for women victims of violence and discrimination, as well as criminal acts of trafficking in persons;
d. eliminate all forms of violence and discrimination against women;
e. provide services to women Victims of violence, and witnesses; and
f. carry out recovery of women Victims of violence to increase the participation of women both individually and in groups as potential and resources in the implementation of social community life.
3. The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:
a. women's rights;
b. obligations and responsibilities of regional government;
c. women's empowerment;
d. women's protection;
e. protection of women victims of violence;
f. prohibitions and sanctions;
g. community participation;
h. monitoring and evaluation; and
i . funding.

Article 3

1. Every woman has the right to:
a. live and maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue offspring;
c. develop her personality, to obtain education, and improve her quality of life to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process;
f. be free from slavery or enslavement and threats;
g. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
h. obtain welfare and a decent life;
i. participate in politics;
j. perform legal acts;
k. freely choose a partner in her marriage; and
l. receive fair wages in accordance with labor regulations.
2. The right to participate in politics as referred to in paragraph (1) letter i is, women have the right to:
a. vote and/or be elected in general elections, regional head elections, or elections for political positions and other positions based on equal rights through direct, general, free, secret voting; and
b. be appointed to every government position.
3. Every woman who is in a condition of disability, elderly, and/or pregnant, has the right to obtain facilities and special treatment.
4. Every elderly woman, person with physical disability and/or mental disability, has the right to obtain care, non-formal education.

Article 4

Every woman with disabilities has the right to obtain:
a. education at all units, paths, types, and levels of education;
b. work and decent livelihood according to the type and degree of disability, education, and ability;
c. equal treatment to play a role in development and enjoy its results;
d. accessibility in the context of her independence;
e. rehabilitation, social assistance, and maintenance of social welfare standards; and
f. equal rights to develop talents, abilities, and social life.

Article 5

Every woman victim of violence has:
a. the right to be respected for her dignity as a human being;
b. the right to recovery of health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the Victim;
c. the right to determine her own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to compensation;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right of the Victim and her family to obtain facilities in the judicial process; and
j. the right to assistance.

CHAPTER V

WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT

Article 8

Women's empowerment is directed to obtain opportunities and rights as human beings to be able to play a role and participate in the fields of:
a. economy;
b. social culture;
c. politics and government;
d. law;
e. education;
f. health;
g. employment; and
h. social security.

Article 9

The implementation of women's empowerment in the economic field as referred to in Article 8 letter a shall be carried out through:
a. provision of skills and job training;
b. facilitation of the formation of productive economic business groups;
c. facilitation of strengthening and development of productive economic business groups;
d. facilitation and capital assistance; and
e. facilitation of marketing network development.

Article 10

Women's empowerment in the social cultural field as referred to in Article 8 letter b shall be carried out through:
a. improvement of knowledge, attitudes and skills to encourage the fulfillment of tiered education in accordance with potential to improve social status;
b. improvement of awareness, willingness and ability to overcome health problems through quality promotive, preventive, curative, and rehabilitative efforts, especially in the field of reproductive health;
c. improvement of awareness and knowledge about independent, healthy and prosperous family planning including access to consultation services and marriage registration; and
d. facilitation and efforts to preserve customs and development of science, technology, arts and culture for the advancement of women.

Article 11

The implementation of women's empowerment in the field of politics and government as referred to in Article 8 letter c includes:
a. involvement of women in decision making;
b. provision of opportunities for women to hold public office;
c. participation in general elections; and
d. self-development through organizations to associate, assemble and express opinions.

Article 17

The Mayor in implementing policies, programs and activities for women's protection undertakes efforts to:
a. coordinate the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for women's protection among regional apparatus;
b. cooperate with regencies/cities within one province and cooperation between regencies/cities in other provinces in the implementation of policy programs, and activities for women's protection in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
c. strengthen institutional capacity for gender mainstreaming for the implementation of policy programs and activities for women's protection;
d. facilitate women's protection services;
e. provide women's protection services;
f. implement affirmative action for women's protection; and
g. prepare a data system for women's protection.

Article 18

The implementation of women's protection is provided to:
a. female workers/laborers;
b. elderly women;
c. women with disabilities;
d. homeless women;
e. domestic workers;
f. women as family economic supporters; and
g. women inmates of correctional institutions.

Article 23

1. Implementation of protection for homeless women in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.
2. Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall receive protection rights over:
a. decent housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunity to obtain skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

Article 33

To provide Protection as referred to in Article 27, the City Regional Government may undertake efforts to:
a. provide services and handling as quickly as possible to witnesses and/or victims;
b. provide convenience, comfort, safety and free of charge for witnesses and/or victims;
c. maintain confidentiality of witnesses and/or victims;
d. guarantee justice and legal certainty for witnesses and/or victims;
e. provide special service rooms at Police Offices;
f. provide apparatus, health workers, social workers, and spiritual counselors;
g. create and develop a system and mechanism for cooperation of service programs involving parties that are easily accessible to victims;
h. provide protection for companions, witnesses, family and friends of victims;
i. provide protection for female workers/laborers in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
